ASM23 - Self-Hosting 6502 Assembler
====================================

Architecture
------------

Two-pass assembler with hash tables for labels and instructions.

  Pass 1: Collect labels and macro definitions. Record forward references.
  Pass 2: Resolve all references and emit code.

The pass is tracked via PASS: $00 = pass 1, $FF = pass 2. Code tests bit 7
(BIT PASS / BMI) to distinguish passes.


Register Conventions
--------------------

  X - Output file handle. Preserved across most calls.
  Y - General-purpose index. Usually clobbered.
  A - Accumulator. Clobbered unless documented otherwise.


Memory Map
----------

  $0000-$00FF   Zero page variables (allocated via .zeropage sections)
  $0100-$01FF   6502 hardware stack
  $0200-$03FF   Forward reference list (512 bytes)
  $0400-$04FF   Label scope stack for macro expansions (256 bytes)
  $0500-$05FF   Macro argument buffer (256 bytes)
  $0600-$067F   TOKEN buffer (128 bytes)
  $0680-$068F   ELSE_SEEN_ARRAY (16 bytes, one per .ifdef nesting level)
  $0700-$07FF   LHASHTAB - Label hash table (128 two-byte entries)
  $0800-$08FF   IFDEF_DECISIONS buffer (256 bytes)
  $2000+        Code section:
                  IHASHTAB (instruction hash table, from inst.asm.out)
                  Instruction/directive data (from inst.asm.out)
                  Assembler code
                  HEAP (immediately after code, grows upward via MEMP16)
  $F000         SOURCE_STACK (grows downward via SS_P16)
  $FFFC-$FFFF   Reset and interrupt vectors

The heap (MEMP16) grows upward storing hash entries, macro definitions, and
forward references. The source stack (SS_P16) grows downward storing
include/macro source contexts. A 256-byte safety buffer is maintained between
them for indexed addressing; err_out_of_memory is raised on collision.


Key Global State
----------------

  CURR_CHAR               Last character read by read_char
                           (alias for SS_CURR_CHAR)
  TOKEN            $0600  Buffer holding current token being parsed
  PASS                    $00 = pass 1, $FF = pass 2
  PC16                    Current program counter
  MEMP16                  Heap pointer (grows upward)
  SS_P16                  Source stack pointer (grows downward)
  HEX16 / OPERAND16      Current parsed value (aliased names)
  IS_FWDREF               $FF if current value is a forward reference
  INST_PTR16 /            Instruction table entry or macro definition
    MACRO_DEF_PTR16        pointer (aliased)
  IN_MACRO_DEF            $FF while capturing macro body
  LABEL_SCOPE16           Current scope identifier for local labels

All are zero page except TOKEN.


Parsing Model
-------------

  - read_char advances input and stores the result in both A and CURR_CHAR.
  - Token reading fills the TOKEN buffer with a null terminator.
  - Single-character lookahead via CURR_CHAR drives parsing decisions.
  - check_for_end_of_line skips spaces and checks for end-of-line or comment.


Hash Tables
-----------

Two hash tables are used, both with 128 two-byte entries (256 bytes each):

IHASHTAB (Instruction hash table) -- Pre-generated by instgen.asm and
included as out/inst.asm.out. Contains mnemonics, addressing mode/opcode
data, and directive handler addresses. Read-only at runtime; no initialization
needed.

LHASHTAB (Label hash table) -- Built at runtime during assembly. Stores
global labels, local labels, macro definitions, and macro parameters. Entries
are allocated on the heap.

Hash entries on the heap have the format:

  [next_ptr_lo] [next_ptr_hi] [key...] $00 [value_lo] [value_hi]

Local and macro labels use an escape format with type byte and scope:

  [next_ptr_lo] [next_ptr_hi] [type] [scope_lo] [scope_hi] [name...] $00
    [value_lo] [value_hi]

common.asm provides select_label_hash_table and
select_instruction_hash_table to switch between the two tables.


Instruction Table Generation
-----------------------------

instgen.asm is a separate program (assembled by the previous version's
assembler) that reads instruction and directive definitions from
instruction_tables.asm, builds a hash table in memory, and outputs assembly
source code (out/inst.asm.out) that recreates the table as static data.

The assembler includes this generated file first (at $2000) so the tables
sit on a page boundary. This avoids runtime initialization and keeps the
assembler smaller.


Forward References
------------------

When pass 1 encounters a label reference before the label is defined, it
records the current PC in the forward reference list (FWDREF_LIST,
$0200-$03FF). The list is an array of 16-bit PC values terminated by $FFFF.

In pass 2, check_forward_ref compares the current PC against the next entry
in the list. When they match, the assembler forces absolute addressing mode
even if the resolved value would fit in zero page. This ensures pass 2 emits
the same instruction sizes as pass 1.


Macro System
------------

Definition (macro_capture.asm): The .macro directive stores parameter names
and the macro body on the heap. During capture, the body is compressed
(comments stripped, consecutive spaces collapsed, string/char literals
preserved verbatim). Nested .macro directives are rejected. In pass 2, the
body is skipped since it was already captured in pass 1.

Expansion (macro_expansion.asm): Macro invocation parses arguments in the
caller's scope, then pushes a new label scope with a unique expansion ID
(EXPANSION_ID16). Parameters are added to the scope's hash table. The macro
body is set up as a memory source on the source stack, so read_char reads from
it transparently.

Scoping: Each expansion gets a unique scope ID from the monotonic
EXPANSION_ID16 counter. Local labels (.name) within a macro are scoped to
that expansion. Parameters shadow global labels of the same name.

Recursion detection: check_macro_recursion walks the scope stack comparing
macro entry addresses. Direct and indirect recursion are both caught.


Source Stack
----------

The source stack (source_stack.asm) handles two source types transparently:

  File sources (type 0): Read from a file handle via the emulator's I/O.
    Used for the main input and .include files.

  Memory sources (type 1): Read from a null-terminated buffer on the heap.
    Used for macro expansion.

Each stack frame stores the source name, type, and enough context to restore
the parent source when the current one is exhausted. Memory frames pushed by
the macro expansion code carry an additional 5-byte activation payload
(LABEL_SCOPE16, CACHED_HASH, MACRO_ENTRY16) right after prev_data. The
assembler installs pop_label_scope_from_frame as the memory-pop handler
(via ss_install_memory_pop at startup); the hook reads the payload back
when a macro frame is popped, restoring the caller's scope.


Conditional Assembly
--------------------

.ifdef, .ifndef, .else, and .endif control conditional assembly. Nesting up
to 16 levels is supported. When a condition is false, the assembler enters
skipping mode (SKIP_DEPTH > 0) where only conditional directives are
processed and all other content is ignored.

IFDEF_DECISIONS records pass 1 decisions so pass 2 can replay them
identically, even for labels that are now defined due to pass 1 collection.


Code Organization
-----------------

asm.asm is the top-level file. It defines memory layout constants, zero page
variables, includes all modules, and contains the assembly loop and entry
point.


Modules (included from asm.asm, in include order):

  out/inst.asm.out    Pre-generated instruction/directive hash table
  environment.asm     Emulator I/O entry points: file ops, console, args
  macros.asm          16-bit utility macros (INC16, CP16, CMP16, PUSH16, ...)
  common.asm          Mode constants, label types, heap; includes hash_table.asm
  label_scope.asm     Scope stack for macro expansions, expansion ID counter
  forward_ref.asm     Forward reference list operations
  source_stack.asm      Unified file/memory source stack, read_char
  errors.asm          Error messages and handlers
  from_decimal.asm    Decimal string to 16-bit value conversion
  output.asm          PC management, byte emission
  tokenizer.asm       Character classification, token/hex reading, escapes
  expressions.asm     Expression evaluation, char literals, byte selectors
  labels.asm          Label capture, local labels, value assignment
  instructions.asm    Instruction lookup, operand parsing, emission
  directives.asm      Directive dispatch, data directives, conditionals
  macro_capture.asm   .macro directive processing, body capture
  macro_expansion.asm Macro invocation, argument parsing, scope setup
  init.asm            CLI argument processing, input file opening

Foundational modules (environment, macros, common, source stack, errors) come
first, followed by parsing and assembly modules, with init last.


Assembly Loop
-------------

The main loop in assemble_code processes input line by line:

  1. Read first character of line. EOF ends the pass.
  2. If capturing a macro body (IN_MACRO_DEF): delegate to capture_macro_line.
  3. If in skipping mode (SKIP_DEPTH > 0): only process conditional directives.
  4. Otherwise: capture label if line starts in column 0, then process
     directive or instruction/macro.


Configuration Aliases
---------------------

Several symbols are configured before their include file to customize
behavior:

  SS_NAME        = TOKEN              Source stack uses TOKEN for filenames
  read_char          = source_stack_read_char
  CURR_CHAR          = SS_CURR_CHAR
  HT_KEY             = TOKEN              Hash table key source
  HT_V16             = HEX16              Hash table value source


Debug Build
-----------

Assembling with define:enable_debug includes additional code:

  - Heap usage and forward reference count reporting
  - show_macros support for displaying captured macro definitions
  - small_heap argument for memory limit testing
  - Forward reference tracking verification between passes
